    "summary": "Solid early‑89 show with strong second set and debut of new songs",
    "blurb": "The band opens with the fresh “Touch of Grey” suite and rolls into a high‑energy second set highlighted by “Playing In The Band,” “He’s Gone,” and an extended “Morning Dew.” Standout moments include the debut of “We Can Run/Standing On The Moon” and a powerful vocal/guitar performance from Jerry.",
    "review": "Feb 5, 1989 at the Henry J. Kaiser Convention Center captures the Dead in a transitional phase – new material is still finding its footing while the classic fire of the late‑‘80s shines through. The first set feels a little uneven; “Touch of Grey” → “Feel Like A Stranger” → “Franklin’s Tower” is crisp but some listeners note a flat tone on “Touch.” However, the band quickly regains momentum with a confident “Althea” and the debut of “We Can Run,” followed by a gritty “Desolation Row.”\n\nThe second set is where the night truly lifts. “Cold Rain And Snow” launches into a tight “Man Smart, Woman Smarter,” then the brand‑new “Standing On The Moon” lands with gusto. A soaring “Playing In The Band” jam feeds into an inspired drum/space segment before Jerry’s emotive vocal and lead work on “He’s Gone.” The set closes with “I Need A Miracle” and a slow‑burning, two‑chorus “Morning Dew,” which many reviewers call a highlight of the year. An encore of “Johnny B. Goode” caps off a show that, despite some patchy pacing, delivers several must‑hear moments for any Deadhead.",
